 Prediction intervals widened as rainfall intensity increased, but the additional width was insufficient to maintain nominal coverage for the strongest events. This result shows that near-nominal \textcolor{red}{95\%} coverage over the complete test distribution can conceal substantial undercoverage in a less frequent but meteorologically important regime. Reporting only overall coverage would therefore give an incomplete assessment of probabilistic forecast reliability.
 
-The Gaussian likelihood also imposes a structural limitation on the uncertainty estimates. Precipitation is non-negative and strongly right-skewed, whereas the Gaussian distribution is symmetric and unbounded. In the single-run diagnostic, the lower bound of the nominal 95\% interval extended below 0~mm\,h$^{-1}$ throughout the test set. Negative interval bounds do not correspond to physically meaningful precipitation and indicate a mismatch between the assumed predictive distribution and the support of the target variable. The Gaussian formulation remains a baseline. The controlled Bernoulli--Gamma comparison removed negative bounds and improved overall probabilistic scores, but substantial upper-tail undercoverage remained. This comparison was limited to Probabilistic BiLSTM-Attention; it does not establish the same benefit for other architectures, test periods or event-detection rules.
+The Gaussian likelihood also imposes a structural limitation on the uncertainty estimates. Precipitation is non-negative and strongly right-skewed, whereas the Gaussian distribution is symmetric and unbounded. In the single-run diagnostic, the lower bound of the nominal 95\% interval extended below 0~mm\,h$^{-1}$ throughout the test set. Negative interval bounds do not correspond to physically meaningful precipitation and indicate a mismatch between the assumed predictive distribution and the support of the target variable. The Gaussian formulation remains a baseline. The controlled Bernoulli--Gamma comparison removed negative bounds and improved overall probabilistic scores, but substantial upper-tail undercoverage remained. This comparison was limited to Probabilistic BiLSTM-Attention; it does not establish the same benefit for other architectures, test periods or event-detection rules. \revminor{Bernoulli--Gamma distributions have previously been used in neural-network-based precipitation downscaling to jointly represent precipitation occurrence and positive amounts \citep{cannon2008bernoulli,bano2020configuration}. In related precipitation downscaling experiments, deterministic predictions slightly underestimated the 98th precipitation percentile, while stochastic sampling improved this aspect at the expense of some temporal and spatial correlation \citep{bano2020configuration}. Although percentile bias and prediction-interval coverage are distinct diagnostics, these findings underscore the importance of evaluating high-intensity precipitation performance separately from aggregate probabilistic skill.}
 
